+*   **Shading:** Gouraud shading is used [S2, unofficial].
 
-**Pitfalls and Edge Cases:**
-*   **Hardware Resolution Limit:** Hardware compositing typically uses 8-bit intensity/opacity resolution. If a small cell contributes less than $1/256$ of the maximum possible intensity, it may be completely ignored by the hardware blending logic, even though the method attempts to avoid ignoring data [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Depthless Weighting:** Small cells contribute the same intensity as large cells because depth is not considered. On grids where cell size is inversely proportional to interest (finely gridded in areas of interest), this automatic weighting may be preferred by some scientists, but it ignores physical depth weighting [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Visual Artifacts:** Noticeable visual artifacts appear from certain angles because the distance between cell faces is not taken into account. These artifacts tend to delineate cell boundaries [S2, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Small Cells:** Small cells contribute the same intensity as large cells because depth is not considered [S2, unofficial]. On grids where cell size is inversely proportional to interest (finely gridded in areas of interest), this automatic weighting may be preferred by some scientists [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Hardware Compositing Limitations:** Hardware compositing uses 8-bit intensity/opacity resolution [S1]. Small cells may contribute well under 1/256 of the maximum possible intensity and thus never appear in the image if depth were considered [S1][S2, unofficial]. The depthless method avoids ignoring data entirely but weights it improperly [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Visual Artifacts:** Noticeable visual artifacts appear from some angles because the distance between cell faces is not taken into account [S2, unofficial]. These artifacts tend to delineate cell boundaries and are unlikely to be misinterpreted as data information [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
